{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 11,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.069,
            0.817,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "103"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.191
          ],
          "content": "Is this a totally convincing explanation? No, we have only given examples where our guess holds true. A statement may be true on a large number of examples, but it does not mean the statement is true in general. So let us explain why the statement is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.195,
            0.886,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 6:** Chords of a circle having the same length are all at the same distance from the centre of the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.247,
            0.443,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "Draw the figure (Fig. 5.14)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.288,
            0.432,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.263,
                0.507,
                0.343,
                0.525
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.14"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.49,
            0.311,
            0.878,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "**Given:** Circle with centre C AB = FG; E, H are midpoints of AB, FG respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.375,
            0.695,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "**To show:** CE = CH."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.4,
            0.877,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "**Why is this true?** From Theorem 5, CE and CH are the perpendiculars from C to the chords AB and FG respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.541,
            0.886,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "So, CE and CH are the distances from the centre C to the chords AB and FG respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.59,
            0.691,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "We shall explain this result in two different ways."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.618,
            0.603,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "(1) Triangles CAB and CFG are congruent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.647,
            0.882,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": "CA = CF (both are equal to the radius of the circle). Likewise, CB = CG."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.675,
            0.881,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": "We are given that $\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{FG}.\\ \\mathrm{SO},$ , by the SSS congruence, $\\Delta \\mathrm { C A B } \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm { C F G }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.704,
            0.886,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": "Hence the altitudes of the congruent triangles are congruent. CE = CH. Therefore, the chords AB and FG are equidistant from the centre C."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.772,
            0.539,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "(2) Consider triangles CEA and CHF."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.801,
            0.886,
            0.843
          ],
          "content": "From Theorem 5, the perpendicular from the centre bisects the chord."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.849,
            0.886,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "Hence, E and H are the midpoints of the chords AB and FG respectively."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
